  <content>
  <section><title>Factoring the GCF from Polynomials</title>
<para>It is sometimes useful to represent a number as a product of factors, for example, 12 as \(2 \cdot 6\) or \(3 \cdot
  4\). In algebra, it can also be useful to represent a polynomial in factored form. We will start with a product, such
  as \(3x^2+15x\), and end with its factors, \(3x(x + 5)\). To do this, we apply the Distributive Property “in
  reverse.” We state the Distributive Property here just as you saw it in earlier chapters and “in
  reverse.”</para>
<note>
  <para class="os-raise-text-bold"> DISTRIBUTIVE PROPERTY </para>
  <hr/>
  <para>If \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) are real numbers, then</para>
  <para>\(\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;\;a(b + c) = ab + ac\) and \(ab + ac = a(b + c)\)</para>
  <para>The form on the left is used to multiply. The form on the right is used to factor.</para>
</note>

<para>So how do you use the Distributive Property to factor a polynomial? You just find the GCF of all the terms and write
the polynomial as a product!</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 1</para>
<para>Factor: \(8m^3-12m^2n+20mn^2\).</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">How to factor the greatest common factor from a polynomial:</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 - </emphasis>Find the GCF of all the terms of the polynomial. For this problem, find the GCF of \(8m^3\), \(12m^2n\), and
  \(20mn^2\).</para>
<para><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2af0ae8a08f2a69dfa71f2cf930dc371ca3a0761"/></media></figure></para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Rewrite each term as a product using the GCF. In this case, rewrite \(8m^3\), \(12m^2n\), and \(20mn^2\) as products
  of their GCF, \(4m\).</para>
<list>
  <item> \(8m^3 = {\style{color:red}4}
    {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 2m^2\) </item>
  <item> \(12m^2n = {\style{color:red}4}
    {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 3mn\) </item>
  <item> \(20mn^2 = {\style{color:red}4}
    {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 5n^2\) </item>
</list>
<para>\(8m^3-12m^2n+20mn^2\)
  \({\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 2m^2 - {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 3mn + {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}m} \cdot 5n^2\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 - </emphasis>Use the “reverse” Distributive Property to factor the expression.</para>
<para>\(4m(2m^2-3mn+5n^2)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4 -</emphasis> Check by multiplying the factors.</para>
<para>\(4m(2m^2-3mn+5n^2)\)
  \(4m \cdot 2m^2 - 4m \cdot 3mn + 4m \cdot 5n^2\)
  \(8m^3-12m^2n+20mn^2\)</para>
<para>When the leading coefficient is negative, we factor the negative out as part of the GCF.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 2</para>
<para>Factor: \(−4a^3 + 36a^2 − 8a\).</para>
<para>The leading coefficient is negative, so the GCF will be negative.</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Rewrite each term using the GCF, \(−4a\).</para>
<para> \(
  {\style{color:red}-}
  {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}a} \cdot a^2 −
  {\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}-}
  {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}a}
  {\style{color:red})} \cdot 9a +
  {\style{color:red}(}{\style{color:red}-}
  {\style{color:red}4}
  {\style{color:red}a}
  {\style{color:red})} \cdot 2\) </para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Factor the GCF.</para>
<para> \(−4a(a^2 − 9a + 2)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3 -</emphasis> Check.</para>
<para> \(−4a(a^2 − 9a + 2)\)
  \(−4a \cdot a^2 − (−4a) \cdot 9a + (−4a) \cdot 2\)
  \(−4a^3 + 36a^2 − 8a\)</para>
<para>So far, our greatest common factors have been monomials. In the next example, the greatest common factor is a
  binomial.</para>
<para class="os-raise-text-bold">Example 3</para>
<para>Factor: \(3y
  {\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}y} \;{\style{color:red}+}\;     {\style{color:red}7}
  {\style{color:red})}
  − 4{\style{color:red}(}
  {\style{color:red}y}\;     {\style{color:red}+} \;{\style{color:red}7}
  {\style{color:red})}\).</para>
<para>The GCF is the binomial \(y + 7\).</para>
<para>\(3y(y + 7) − 4(y + 7)\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1 -</emphasis> Factor the GCF, \((y + 7)\).</para>
<para> \((y + 7)(3y − 4)\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2 -</emphasis> Check on your own by multiplying.</para>

<title>Try It: Factoring the GCF from Polynomials</title>
<para>Factor.</para>
<example><exercise>
  <problem>
    
    <para> \(9xy^2 + 6x^2y^2 + 21y^3\) </para>
    <para> \(−4b^3 + 16b^2 − 8b\) </para>
    <para> \(4m(m + 3) − 7(m + 3)\) </para>
    <para>Write down your answers. Then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
  </problem><solution>
  <para>Here is how to factor the GCF from polynomials.</para>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> \(3y^2(3x + 2x^2 + 7y)\) </item>
  </list>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
    <item> \(−4b(b^2 − 4b + 2)\) </item>
  </list>
  <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
    <item> \((m + 3)(4m − 7)\) </item>
  </list>
</solution></exercise>
</example></section>
